## The product

Employees book a session with a mental-health practitioner and meet over live chat,
voice or video. That is two products really: the flow that gets someone booked, and the
room the session actually happens in.

## Real-time sessions

A dropped call in this context is not a cosmetic bug. Sessions have to survive a shaky
connection, reconnect without losing the thread of the conversation, and fall back to
voice or chat when video will not hold. Presence has to be accurate on both sides too,
because a practitioner sitting in an empty room and a patient who thinks nobody showed
up are the same failure viewed from two ends.

## Booking

Booking looks simple from the outside and is where most of the constraints live.
Practitioner availability, timezones, session length and double-booking all have to be
enforced on the server, because the client cannot be the authority on whether a slot is
still free. Two people requesting the last slot in the same second is ordinary traffic,
not an edge case, and the database is the only place that argument can be settled.

## Sensitive by default

Mental-health sessions are close to the most private thing a system can hold. Access is
scoped so people reach only their own records, and the surfaces that touch session
content are kept few and deliberate. Reliability and privacy were treated as product
requirements from the start rather than hardening applied at the end.

## Stack

Python and Django REST, React and Next.js, on AWS. Two-week sprints with a distributed
team, reporting to stakeholders directly.
